Discovery job ownership and safe status responses
================================================

POST /network/discovery/start and POST /network/topology/refresh reserve a
random UUID with its organization, optional site and exact discovery task kind
before publishing the task. Redis SET NX prevents replacement; the record
expires after 24 hours. The existing Sentinel-aware client factory is used.
A store failure or reservation collision refuses publication. If broker
acknowledgement fails, the API retains the owner and returns HTTP 503 with the
same task ID and an explicit unknown-outcome message. Inspect that ID before
retrying: this is not durable publication recovery or exactly-once execution.

GET /network/discovery/status requires the separate ownership record. A task ID,
Celery result or generic progress record alone never grants access. The caller
must have the current organization context and current site grant; a named site
must still be active, not deleted and in that organization. This applies even
to administrators using a selected organization. Site-limited callers cannot
read organization-wide jobs. Existing device-read/admin permissions still apply.

The result response contains only task ID, a known state, fixed message, bounded
integer discovered-device count, coarse completion percentage and null timing
fields. It does not expose controller diagnostics, tracebacks or arbitrary result
fields. The real aggregate total_devices_discovered takes precedence over the
legacy devices_found field. Explicit success:false maps to failure; malformed
counts/backend states return a safe unavailable response. This boundary does not
redefine partial-controller success semantics in the discovery worker.

Upgrade and operational limits
------------------------------

Legacy and expired IDs without the new ownership record return 404, even when a
Celery result remains. Scheduled maintenance tasks are not tenant-submitted jobs
and do not receive a public status owner. A job queued for more than 24 hours can
lose status visibility. Redis eviction or loss also removes visibility; there is
no database recovery or legacy ownership backfill. Deploy all API replicas
consistently: an older replica retains the unsafe legacy result access path.

Authorization is checked at read/submission time; this does not fence a running
hardware operation if authorization changes afterward. Zero explicit site grants
retain the existing unrestricted-within-organization policy. Raw Celery result
storage and infrastructure logs remain sensitive. Discovery lock ownership,
controller aggregation, controller error persistence and device identity matching
are separate audit work. No multi-host failover or hardware write qualification
is claimed by these tests.

Verification
------------

Boundary regressions reproduce cross-tenant/unowned reads on the preceding code.
Store and projection tests cover malformed records, cancellation, closure,
non-overwriting reservation and diagnostic suppression. Real PostgreSQL tests
apply site disable/delete/move changes in separate committed transactions.
Release probes additionally use real Redis/Valkey, API routes and a separate
Celery worker executing the shipping task wrapper. Authentication and the
controller-discovery coroutine are synthetic, so these probes do not contact
hardware or qualify adapter behavior. They exercise acknowledgement loss after
actual broker acceptance and observe ownership before the synthetic effect.
